  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will gradually exit to the east of the \nNorthern Rockies through the late morning and afternoon hours, with \nincreasing westerly winds replacing the departing precipitation for \nthe remainder of the day. High temperatures in wake a Pacific cold \nfront will only rise into the mid-50s to near 70 degrees today, \nwhich is 10 to 15 degrees below normal. Falling snow levels along \nthe Continental Divide, specifically the Glacier National Park \nregion, is expected to lead to some minor snowfall accumulations \nprior to precipitation ending.\n\nQuasi-zonal flow to begin the work week will back to the southwest \nthrough the day on Tuesday as upper level troughing digs south along \nthe western seaboard. This trough will deepen through the middle of \nthe work week while slowly drifting east over the Western CONUS, \nwhich will place the Northern Rockies beneath southwest to southerly \nflow through the remainder of the forecast period. Pacific moisture \nstreaming northeast to north within this flow will support daily \nchances for showers and storms from Tuesday through the upcoming \nweek, especially over the mountains of Southwest Montana and along \nthe Continental Divide. Temperatures will see-saw between 5 degrees \nabove to below normal through the upcoming work week within this \npattern, with periods of gusty south winds developing in the north-\nsouth orientated valleys of Southwest Montana as the upper level \nflow becomes more southerly in nature. - Moldan\n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nGusty Winds through the day today...\n\nConfidence remains high that gusty west winds will develop across \nall of Southwest through North Central Montana today, most notably \nfrom the late morning through evening hours. There is high \nconfidence that frequent wind gusts of between 30-40 mph will occur \nacross the region through this timeframe, with the strongest winds \noccurring along the Rocky Mountain Front west of the I-15 corridor \nand along the Montana Hwy 200/US Hwy 87 corridors between Great \nFalls and Lewistown. One notable change from the past 48 hours has \nbeen a northward shift in the strongest H850-700 winds, which are \nnow prog'd to reside between the Montana Hwy 200 and US Hwy 2 \ncorridors. This northward shift from the past 48 hours, which had \nbeen showing the core of strongest winds residing over the Montana \nHwy 200 corridor, has resulted in an increase (albeit still low) in \nprobabilities for wind gusts to exceed 55 mph along the US Hwy 2 \ncorridor. It is not out of the realm of possibility that a few wind \nprone locations across Glacier County and in lee of the Sweet Grass \nHills will reach or exceed High Wind criteria today; however, given \nthat these occurrences will be far and few between we will continue \nto hold off on issuing any High Wind Warnings. None-the-less, those \ntraveling in high profile vehicles should be prepared for strong \ncross winds.\n\nListed below are the NBM5.0 probabilities for wind gusts to exceed \n35, 45, and 55 mph for select locations across North Central, \nCentral, and Southwest Montana.\n\n                     Sunday's Wind Gusts Probabilities\nLOCATION               > 35 mph | > 45 mph | > 55 mph\nBrowning                 100%   |    85%   |    20% \nCut Bank                 100%   |    85%   |    25%\nHavre                     95%   |    50%   |    10%\nGreat Falls               95%   |    50%   |    <5%\nLewistown                 65%   |     5%   |     0%\nHelena                    80%   |    15%   |    <5%\nBozeman                   40%   |     5%   |    <5%\nDillon                    60%   |     5%   |     0%\nEnnis                     50%   |     5%   |     0%\nWest Yellowstone          75%   |    <5%   |     0%\n\n\nAccumulating Snow in the Mountains of Glacier National Park...\n\nConfidence in minor snowfall accumulations has increased for the \nmountains of Glacier National Park, mainly for those elevations \nabove 6500ft. Model guidance has settled in on better timing of \nprecipitation moving across the park as snow level fall, with this \noverlap between both precipitation and \"low\" snow levels promoting \nsome minor accumulations of up to 1-2\" for the higher peaks. Those \nlooking to recreate in the backcountry of Glacier National Park above \n6500ft should be prepared for wet and raw conditions, along with the \npotential for slippery trail conditions. - Moldan",
